# Subagents and context rot

Long chats fill with logs, tool dumps, and false trails (**context rot**). Subagents exist primarily for **context isolation**, not for looking busy.

## Main agent keeps

- User requirements, constraints, decisions
- The current plan and acceptance criteria
- Final code edits that need full project judgment
- One-line status to the user

## Delegate to a subagent when

- Tailing / holding a long suite streak or gate revive (noisy, long-running)
- Exploring unfamiliar code or grepping broadly (“return paths + 5-line summary”)
- Running a tight iso loop that prints a lot (return PASS N/N + log path only)
- Parallel **read-only** threads (two independent investigations)

## Do not delegate when

- A 30-second local command answers the question
- Two agents would edit the **same files** in parallel (serialize writes)
- The brief would be incomplete — cold subagents know only what you write

## Brief quality (mandatory)

Every subagent prompt must be **self-contained**: goal, constraints, paths, IP/MACs if fixture, done criteria, and **exact deliverable shape** (e.g. `SUCCESS 10/10 | STOP at N: case + error + ONE next fix`).

## After a subagent returns

- Trust the compact verdict; **do not** paste their transcript into the user chat
- Take **one** next action (fix / gate / hold / ask user)
- Link cloud subagents as `[Name](bc-id)` when mentioning them
